  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Number theory questions on hard ACT items test divisibility rules, prime factorisation, GCD and LCM, modular arithmetic, and properties of integers.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "GCD",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "greatest common divisor",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "LCM",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\text{LCM}(a,b)=\\dfrac{ab}{\\gcd(a,b)} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Modular arithmetic",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( a \\equiv r \\pmod{n} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Prime factorisation",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "unique decomposition theorem",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 1.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Find LCM(12, 18). \\[ 12=2^2\\cdot 3,\\quad 18=2\\cdot 3^2.\\quad \\text{LCM}=2^2\\cdot 3^2=36. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "<h3>Complex numbers revisited</h3>On advanced ACT items, complex numbers appear in equation-solving contexts: \\( x^2+4=0 \\Rightarrow x=\\pm 2i \\). The modulus of \\( a+bi \\) is \\( \\sqrt{a^2+b^2} \\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Sequences & Modular Arithmetic",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "An <strong>arithmetic sequence</strong>: \\(a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\\). A <strong>geometric sequence</strong>: \\(a_n = a_1 r^{n-1}\\). <strong>Modular arithmetic</strong>: \\(a \\equiv b \\pmod{m}\\) means \\(m \\mid (a-b)\\). Useful for finding last digits (mod 10) or remainders.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example — Last digit of a power",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "What is the units digit of \\(7^{2026}\\)? <p>Powers of 7 cycle: \\(7^1=7, 7^2=49, 7^3=343, 7^4=2401, 7^5=\\ldots7\\). Cycle length 4. \\(2026 \\div 4\\) remainder \\(2\\). So units digit = same as \\(7^2\\) = <strong>9</strong>.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "The sum of an arithmetic sequence is 150, with first term 5 and last term 25. How many terms are there?",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\(S = \\dfrac{n}{2}(a_1+a_n) = \\dfrac{n}{2}(30) = 150 \\Rightarrow n = 10\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
